<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12454"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 9:13 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks Lost and Ivy - I don&#39;t like to associate serial killer fiction and fact because the two are so far apart and confuse the general public, but there&#39;s a quote from <i>Red Dragon</i>, the movie version of Thomas Harris&#39;s novel - the one starring Edward Norton and Harvey Keitel, not <i>Manhunter</i> starring William Petersen from the 80&#39;s - that adds to what Ivy leaguer wrote -  <BR> <BR><b>Will Graham:</b> He&#39;s not going to stop. <BR><b>Police officer:</b> Why? <BR><b>Will Graham:</b> <i>Because it makes him God.</i> <BR> <BR>Doesn&#39;t matter if a screenwriter or novelist came up with that exchange - I think it cuts to the heart of what Ivy wrote: <i>&#34;...those roles did not provide the gratification that accompanied murder...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR><b>&#34;Have you seen blood in the moonlight? It appears quite black.&#34; ~ <i>Hannibal Lecter,</i> &#34;Red Dragon.&#34;</b> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
